<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 10px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="font-size: 24px; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <h2 style="font-size: 16px; margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</h2>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> School Life Adventures</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> That's a Big Book!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Identify key words and phrases in texts. Reading: Follow written instructions with visual aids. Phonemic Awareness: Identify a spoken word's initial and final phoneme if these words have been previously encountered and are attached to a picture.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can identify keywords and phrases in simple texts related to classroom objects (e.g., recognizing "desk," "book," and "pen" in the sentence, "The pen is on the desk next to the book."). Can follow written instructions to identify classroom objects (e.g., "Circle the picture of the pencil."). Can identify the initial and final phonemes of common classroom objects and make the correspondence between letters and their sounds at the beginning and end of words with visual support (e.g., identifying the initial letter and sound /p/ and the final letter and sound /l/ in "pencil" when shown a picture of a pencil).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h3>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</p>
                <p style="margin: 0; text-align: justify;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students politely ("Good morning, how are you?") and reviews classroom objects using large flashcards or realia (book, pencil, desk, chair, bag). The teacher holds up a flashcard, says the word, and students repeat chorally and individually. For example, "This is a book. Repeat: book." "This is a pencil. Repeat: pencil." The teacher then asks students to point to specific objects in the classroom: "Point to the chair!" or "Point to the board!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard showing a "big, blue book" and models reading the phrase: "This is a big, blue book." The teacher points to each word as she reads. She then models identifying the initial phoneme of "book" (/b/) and "small" (/s/) using flashcards with the letters clearly visible. For "pencil," she points to the "p" and says "/p/, pencil." For "book," she points to the "b" and says "/b/, book." The teacher also models reading short instructions like "Circle the red pencil." or "Find the big book." by demonstrating on a sample worksheet.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding with simple questions.
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a small book) "Is this a big book?" (Expected answer: "No, it's small.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a blue crayon) "What color is this crayon?" (Expected answer: "It's blue.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a picture of a desk) "Does 'desk' start with /d/?" (Expected answer: "Yes.")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</p>
                <p style="margin: 0; text-align: justify;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a large, visually rich poster titled "My Classroom Adventures" featuring a simple, short narrative text with illustrations of various classroom objects (book, desk, chair, pencil, bag, board). The text includes adjectives, present simple, demonstratives, and prepositions.
                    <br>Example Text on Poster: "This is my classroom! I see a big, green board. That is a small, yellow book. The blue pencil is on the desk. My red bag is under the chair. I have a long ruler and a short eraser. What do you see?"
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Identify key words and phrases in texts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading (Teacher-led):</b> The teacher reads the text aloud once, pointing to each word. Then asks, "What is this text about?" (Expected: "My classroom," "objects"). "Are there books in the classroom?" (Expected: "Yes.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Reading:</b> The teacher reads simple statements based on the text. Students show "True" (thumbs up) or "False" (thumbs down).
                            <br>1. "The board is red." (Students read silently and respond; Expected: False)
                            <br>2. "The book is yellow." (Students read silently and respond; Expected: True)
                            <br>3. "The bag is on the chair." (Students read silently and respond; Expected: False – it's *under* the chair)
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Keyword Identification:</b> The teacher says a word (e.g., "desk"). Students read the text on the poster and point to the word "desk" in the text. Repeat for "book," "pencil," "bag," "chair."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</p>
                <p style="margin: 0; text-align: justify;">(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading classroom object vocabulary (nouns, adjectives), identifying initial/final phonemes, reading and understanding present simple sentences, demonstratives (this, that), and prepositions (on, under, next to, in).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Read and Match (Controlled Practice):</b> Students receive a worksheet with pictures of classroom objects (book, pencil, desk, chair, eraser) on one side and the corresponding words on the other, but mixed up. Students read each word and draw a line to match it to the correct picture. The teacher circulates, checking understanding and providing support.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Read and Circle (Semi-controlled Practice):</b> Students get a worksheet with several pictures on each line (e.g., a big book and a small book). Below each set of pictures, there is a simple written instruction like: "Circle the big book." or "Circle the red pencil." or "Circle the bag under the desk." Students read the instruction and circle the correct picture. This practices identifying keywords and phrases.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Initial/Final Phoneme Read & Identify (Pair Check):</b> In pairs, students receive a worksheet with 4-5 pictures of classroom objects (e.g., 'pencil', 'book', 'desk', 'ruler'). Below each picture, there are two boxes: one for the initial letter and one for the final letter. Students read the word silently, say it aloud, and identify the initial and final phonemes/letters.
                            <br>Example: For 'pencil', they write 'P' in the first box and 'L' in the second. For 'book', they write 'B' and 'K'.
                            <br>After completing, pairs check each other's work by saying the word and pointing to the initial/final letters. Teacher monitors and provides assistance.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 0;">15-17 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</p>
                <p style="margin: 0; text-align: justify;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "My Classroom Map - Read and Draw!" Individual student activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students receive a blank "classroom map" worksheet (a simple outline of a classroom with outlines for a few desks and chairs). They also receive a list of 5-6 written instructions related to placing and drawing classroom objects.
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. "Draw a big, red book on the desk."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. "Draw a small, blue pencil next to the book."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. "Draw a green bag under the chair."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. "Draw a yellow ruler in the bin."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">5. "Draw a long, brown chalk on the board."</li>
                    </ul>
                    Students must read each instruction carefully, identify the keywords (object, adjective, preposition), and then draw the described object in the correct location on their map. The teacher emphasizes reading each instruction completely before drawing. Teacher circulates to offer help and observe students' reading comprehension.
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</p>
                <p style="margin: 0; text-align: justify;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and individual worksheet assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete a short individual assessment worksheet.
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part 1: Read and Follow Instructions:</b> Students read two simple instructions with visual aids. E.g., "Circle the small, yellow eraser." (picture of various erasers) and "Draw a blue book under the desk." (picture of a desk).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part 2: Keyword Identification:</b> Students read a short sentence like "The long ruler is red." and then circle the words "ruler" and "red."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part 3: Phonemic Awareness:</b> Students see pictures of "marker" and "scissors." They write the initial letter for "marker" and the final letter for "scissors" in provided blanks.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identifies keywords and phrases in simple texts (e.g., 'book', 'red', 'desk', 'under').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Follows written instructions with visual aids (e.g., circling correct object, drawing in correct location).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identifies initial and final phonemes/letters of familiar classroom objects (e.g., /m/ for marker, /s/ for scissors).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick self-reflection.
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can read words like 'book' and 'pencil'. (Yes / No / A little)"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can follow instructions like 'Circle the chair'. (Yes / No / A little)"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</p>
                <p style="margin: 0; text-align: justify;">(Teacher and student reflection, connection to next lesson)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b> The teacher asks the class:
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to read today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was hard to read today?"</li>
                    </ul>
                    Students can share their answers by raising hands or showing thumbs up/down for specific words/tasks.
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students identify keywords and follow written instructions?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What were the common challenges students faced in reading or identifying phonemes? What adjustments or additional scaffolds might be needed for students who struggled? (e.g., more visual cues, simpler sentences, paired reading).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How effectively were the target grammar (adjectives, prepositions) and vocabulary integrated into the reading tasks?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "Great job reading about our classroom today! Next time, we will practice talking about these objects and playing games where we describe what we see. We will use the words we read today to speak about 'That's a Big Book!' and other things in our classroom." (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 0;">3-5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>